// Chapter headings and background written for Joined: Helaman through Moroni.
Object.assign(CHAPTERS,{
"hel.1":{title:"There began to be a serious contention",year:"About 52 BC",
 sum:"After Pahoran dies, his sons Pahoran, Paanchi, and Pacumeni contend for the judgment-seat—Pacumeni is appointed, and Kishkumen murders Pahoran and hides among the people—The Lamanite army under Coriantumr takes Zarahemla and kills Pacumeni—Moronihah retakes the city and the Lamanites are driven out.",
 bg:["Kishkumen’s murder of Pahoran is the first act of the secret band that Gadianton will soon lead (1:9–12; 2:4). The men swore a covenant to tell no one, which is how the secret combinations in this book keep their crimes hidden.","Coriantumr is a Nephite dissenter who is “a descendant of Zarahemla” (1:15), and the Lamanites march straight to the capital while the Nephites are busy fighting over the government. Mormon says the attack succeeded because they “had not kept sufficient guards” (1:18), a reminder of how quickly division at the top leaves a people exposed."]},
"hel.2":{title:"The secret work of murder and of robbery",year:"About 50 BC",
 sum:"Helaman, son of Helaman, is appointed chief judge—Kishkumen plots to murder him, but Helaman’s servant, who learned the plan in disguise, stabs Kishkumen first—Gadianton, expert in the secret work of murder and robbery, leads the band into the wilderness—The Nephites rejoice, and Mormon warns that Gadianton will almost destroy the people.",
 bg:["This is the first time Gadianton is named, and Mormon says he was “exceedingly expert in many words,” or a skilled flatterer (2:4–5). Mormon steps in to tell readers that this man will prove “almost the entire destruction of the people of Nephi” (2:13–14), so the reader knows from the start where this story ends.","The Book of Mormon does not explain the meaning of the names Gadianton or Kishkumen, so it is wise not to guess. Later, Mormon will trace the origin of such secret oaths back to Satan, not to any record (6:26–30)."]},
"hel.3":{title:"The gate of heaven is open unto all",year:"About 49–39 BC",
 sum:"A time of small contentions and of pride in the church passes, and many Nephites migrate northward, where they build houses of cement—Helaman governs justly, and his sons are named Nephi and Lehi—There is great prosperity in the church, but pride and persecution return—Mormon testifies that “the gate of heaven is open unto all” and that the word of God leads across the gulf of misery.",
 bg:["Verse 21 says Helaman gave his two sons the names Nephi and Lehi, after the first Nephi and Lehi. Their father will explain in Helaman 5:6–7 that he did it so they would remember their ancestors and “their works,” and try to live up to the name.","Verse 29 speaks of the word of God that is “quick and powerful” and divides asunder the “wiles of the devil,” which echoes Hebrews 4:12. It also recalls Lehi’s dream, where the word of God is like the iron rod that leads over the gulf (1 Nephi 8:19–24; 15:23–24). The Nephites in the north built with cement because timber was scarce (3:7–10); ancient Mesoamericans did use lime plaster and cement, though the text does not say where this land was."]},
"hel.4":{title:"They were left in their own strength",year:"About 38–30 BC",
 sum:"Dissension in the church and war lead dissenters to join the Lamanites—The Lamanites conquer Zarahemla and drive the Nephites to the land Bountiful—Moronihah, Nephi, and Lehi preach, and the people repent and recover part of their land—Pride, oppression of the poor, and rejection of prophets cause the Spirit to withdraw, and the Nephites become “weak, like unto their brethren.”",
 bg:["Mormon names the sins behind the loss: pride, “exceeding riches,” and “oppression to the poor, withholding their food from the hungry” (4:12). He adds that this happened even among those “who professed to belong to the church of God” (4:11), so he is speaking to believers, not only outsiders.","Verse 24 says “the Spirit of the Lord doth not dwell in unholy temples,” as King Benjamin and Amulek also taught (Mosiah 2:37; Alma 34:36). Paul used a similar picture when he told the Corinthians that they are the temple of God (1 Corinthians 3:16–17)."]},
"hel.5":{title:"Upon the rock of our Redeemer",year:"About 30 BC",
 sum:"Nephi gives up the judgment-seat to Cezoram and, with his brother Lehi, preaches the word of God—Helaman had taught them to remember their names and to build upon “the rock of our Redeemer”—Eight thousand Lamanites are baptized, and Nephi and Lehi are cast into prison—They are encircled by fire, a cloud of darkness, a still voice, and angels, and about three hundred are converted.",
 bg:["Helaman explains the sons’ names in verses 6–7: he gave them the names of “our first parents who came out of the land of Jerusalem,” so that remembering their names they would remember their works and try to do good as well. In verse 12 he tells them to build their foundation on Christ, “the rock of our Redeemer,” so the devil’s storms cannot drag them down. Jesus used the same picture of a house on a rock (Matthew 7:24–25), and Paul wrote that “that Rock was Christ” (1 Corinthians 10:4).","The voice in the prison is “a still voice of perfect mildness, as if it had been a whisper,” yet the earth shakes (5:30–31). This recalls Elijah, who found the Lord not in wind or earthquake but in “a still small voice” (1 Kings 19:11–12). The pillar of fire that does not burn them brings to mind the fire in Exodus 3:2 and the pillar of fire in Exodus 13:21."]},
"hel.6":{title:"Gadianton’s robbers and murderers",year:"About 29–23 BC",
 sum:"The Lamanites become more righteous than the Nephites, and for a time Nephites and Lamanites trade and travel freely—The land south is called Lehi and the land north is called Mulek—Pride, riches, and the secret band of Gadianton spread among the Nephites—Mormon says Satan, not the records, put the secret oaths into Gadianton’s heart.",
 bg:["Verse 10 tells us how two regions got their names: the land south was called Lehi and the land north was called Mulek, “after the son of Zedekiah,” as the Lord “did bring Mulek into the land north, and Lehi into the land south.” The name Mulek looks like the Hebrew word for “king” (melek), but the text itself does not say so.","In verses 26–30 Mormon says the same being who enticed Eve and led Cain to murder put the secret oaths in Gadianton’s heart. Compare Moses 5:29–31, where Cain makes a similar secret covenant with Satan. Verse 1 also shows how much the Lamanites’ firm faith had grown, and verse 37 shows them hunting the robbers out of their land."]},
"hel.7":{title:"Pour out my soul unto my God",year:"About 23 BC",
 sum:"Nephi returns from the north after the people there reject his words—He finds Gadianton’s robbers filling the judgment-seats—He prays from his garden tower and a crowd gathers—Nephi calls the people to repent, warns that the Lamanites will fare better than they, and tells them he speaks what the Lord has made known to him.",
 bg:["Nephi’s prayer is a lament: he wishes he had lived in the days when his father Nephi first came to the promised land (7:6–9). Many of the Psalms also pour out grief to God in the same way (for example Psalm 42:3–5). The text says only that the tower stood in his garden by the road to the chief market, so people passing by saw him (7:10–11).","His message in verses 23–24 is that the Lamanites are “more righteous” than the Nephites “for they have not sinned against that great knowledge which ye have received.” The idea that more light means more responsibility appears also in Luke 12:48."]},
"hel.8":{title:"Did he not bear record that the Son of God should come",year:"About 23 BC",
 sum:"Judges who belong to Gadianton’s band accuse Nephi—Nephi teaches that Moses and the brazen serpent testified of Christ and that Abraham, Zenos, Zenock, Isaiah, and Jeremiah also did—He tells them the chief judge has been murdered by his brother and is lying in his blood—All of this is a sign that the people have rejected the Lord.",
 bg:["Nephi says that as Moses “lifted up the brazen serpent in the wilderness, even so shall he be lifted up who should come” (8:14), the same comparison Jesus himself used in John 3:14, looking back at Numbers 21:8–9. Verse 18 says there were prophets “after the order of his Son” before Abraham; see Alma 13 and Doctrine and Covenants 107:3–4 on the priesthood “after the Order of the Son of God.”","Zenos and Zenock are Old Testament prophets whose writings are on the plates of brass but not in our Bible (8:19–20). Zenos is quoted at length in Jacob 5, and Alma 33:3–17 also quotes both of them."]},
"hel.9":{title:"Told us the thoughts of our hearts",year:"About 23 BC",
 sum:"Five men run to the judgment-seat and find the chief judge, Seezoram, murdered—The five are accused, but the truth comes out and they are freed—Nephi is accused of conspiracy and tells the people to go to Seantum, who is Seezoram’s brother—When confronted, Seantum is shown to be the murderer, and some say Nephi is a prophet and others say he is a god.",
 bg:["Nephi’s first sign was the death itself; his second is that the murderer is Seantum, Seezoram’s brother. He tells them to question Seantum and they will find “blood upon the skirts of his cloak” and that fear will make him confess (9:25–36), and it happens just as he said (9:37–38).","In verse 41 some call Nephi “a god.” The same thing happened when Paul healed a man at Lystra (Acts 14:11) and when a viper did Paul no harm on Malta (Acts 28:6). In each case the true message is that God, not the man, is the source."]},
"hel.10":{title:"Whatsoever ye shall seal on earth",year:"About 23–21 BC",
 sum:"Nephi ponders on his way home, and the Lord blesses him for declaring the word without fear—The Lord gives him the sealing power and the power to smite the earth with famine—Nephi is carried by the Spirit from multitude to multitude as the people harden their hearts—The people are divided and begin to slay one another.",
 bg:["The Lord’s promise in verse 7, “whatsoever ye shall seal on earth shall be sealed in heaven,” closely matches Jesus’s words to Peter in Matthew 16:19 and Matthew 18:18. In the Church today it is the same sealing power, as explained in Doctrine and Covenants 132:46. Elijah also used a divine power over the heavens (1 Kings 17:1).","Nephi is “taken by the Spirit and conveyed away out of the midst of them” (10:16), as Philip was after he baptized the Ethiopian (Acts 8:39)."]},
"hel.11":{title:"Let there be a famine",year:"About 20–6 BC",
 sum:"Wars rage across the land, led by the secret band—Nephi prays for a famine instead of the sword, and the Lord sends one—The people repent, and Nephi prays for rain, which comes—Years of peace and prosperity follow, until the robbers of Gadianton rise again and the people soon forget God.",
 bg:["Nephi’s prayer for famine rather than war (11:4) is a hard choice, and the text says he chose it hoping that “perhaps they will repent.” The drought and the rain on his word (11:13–17) recall Elijah, who prayed that it would not rain, then prayed again and it did (1 Kings 17:1; 18:41–45; James 5:17–18).","Mormon says the Gadianton band was “extinct,” but its secret plans had only been hidden in the earth (11:10). It was revived when dissenters took up its oaths (11:24–26). This shows how a sin pulled out of sight can return when the people’s hearts have not changed."]},
"hel.12":{title:"The nothingness of the children of men",year:"About 6 BC",
 sum:"Mormon reflects on how quickly people forget the Lord when they prosper—Men are “less than the dust of the earth,” yet God commands the earth, the sun, the sea, and the mountains—Repentance has been declared so that men might be saved, and all will be judged by their works.",
 bg:["This chapter is Mormon’s own commentary, written as he edits the record, and it reflects on the cycle of pride and forgetting that he has just described. “The nothingness of the children of men” (12:7) uses the same word as King Benjamin’s message that we are nothing compared with God (Mosiah 4:5). It is meant to humble readers, and it also shows how great God’s power is.","In verses 13–15 the earth “goeth back” to lengthen the day, and Mormon adds, “it is the earth that moveth and not the sun.” This is a retelling of when the sun stood still at Joshua’s command (Joshua 10:12–14), and it is a short, plain statement about how the earth moves. Verse 26 closes with the idea that those who have done good will have everlasting life and those who have done evil everlasting damnation, which is close to John 5:29."]},
"hel.13":{title:"The sword of justice hangeth over this people",year:"About 6 BC",
 sum:"Samuel the Lamanite is cast out of Zarahemla, but the Lord tells him to return and he prophesies from the city wall—Unless they repent, destruction will come within four hundred years—The righteous alone preserve the city, and the people’s riches and pride will be cursed—The Nephites are worse than those who stoned the prophets of old, and their days of probation will pass.",
 bg:["Samuel is a Lamanite sent to preach to the Nephites, which reverses the usual direction of the record. Because the people will not let him enter the city, he climbs onto its wall to speak (13:4). His name appears in the Bible as the prophet Samuel, and 1 Samuel 1:20 links it to Hannah’s asking God for a son. Scholars still debate its exact meaning, with “name of God” and “God has heard” the most common suggestions.","The people say they would not have killed the prophets in their fathers’ days (13:25), and Samuel answers “ye are worse than they” (13:26). Jesus said almost exactly the same thing to the scribes and Pharisees in Matthew 23:29–31. The “four hundred years” (13:5, 9) were fulfilled when the Nephite nation fell at Cumorah (Mormon 6; 8:6)."]},
"hel.14":{title:"There shall a new star arise",year:"About 6 BC",
 sum:"Samuel prophesies that in five years the Son of God will be born, with a day and a night and a day without darkness and a new star—He also gives signs of Christ’s death: three days of darkness, thunder, earthquakes, rent rocks, and opened graves—Christ’s resurrection redeems all from the first death—Samuel reminds them, “ye are free; ye are permitted to act for yourselves.”",
 bg:["The sign of the star (14:5) matches the star the wise men saw in Matthew 2:2, and Luke 2:9–14 tells of the angels. The signs of Christ’s death match Matthew 27:45, 51–53, where there was darkness over the land, the earth quaked, rocks were rent, and graves opened. Samuel’s signs are fulfilled in 3 Nephi 1:15–21 and 3 Nephi 8:3–23, where the darkness lasts three days (8:23).","Samuel’s message in verses 30–31 is that no one is forced to fall: “whosoever perisheth, perisheth unto himself.” God has given people a knowledge of good and evil, and the freedom to choose, which also agrees with Lehi’s teaching in 2 Nephi 2:27. The “first death” and “second death” in verses 16–19 are explained in Alma 12:16 and 42:9."]},
"hel.15":{title:"Because he loveth them",year:"About 6 BC",
 sum:"Samuel warns that unless the Nephites repent their houses will be left desolate—The Lord has chastened them because he loves them, but he also loves the Lamanites—Many Lamanites have been converted, buried their weapons of war, and stand firm—The Lord will not utterly destroy the Lamanites, but will bring them back in the latter times.",
 bg:["Samuel says, “the people of Nephi hath he loved, and also hath he chastened them… because he loveth them” (15:3), which agrees with Proverbs 3:12 and Hebrews 12:6, where the Lord corrects those he loves. He also uses the word “hated” of the Lamanites in verse 4, but the reason he gives is their evil deeds and “the tradition of their fathers,” and he adds that salvation has since come to them.","Verse 9 refers to the converted Lamanites, the people of Ammon, who buried their weapons of war and would “suffer themselves that they be trodden down and slain,” as in Alma 24:17–19. Samuel also says the prophet Zenos promised that the Lamanites will be restored in the latter days (15:11–13)."]},
"hel.16":{title:"Great signs given unto the people",year:"About 6–1 BC",
 sum:"Some believe Samuel and are baptized by Nephi, but others stone him and shoot arrows, and he is protected by the Spirit—Samuel escapes to his own land and is not heard of again among the Nephites—In the ninetieth year there are great signs and angels, but the people harden their hearts and say it is not reasonable that a Christ shall come—The book of Helaman ends.",
 bg:["The angels who “glad tidings of great joy” in verse 14 use the same words the angel spoke to the shepherds in Luke 2:10. Samuel stands on the wall while stones and arrows miss him, and the text says this was because “the Spirit of the Lord was with him” (16:2).","In verses 18–20 the unbelievers say if there is a Christ, why will he not show himself in this land as well as at Jerusalem? That question will be answered when the risen Lord appears at the temple in Bountiful (3 Nephi 11). This verse also marks the end of the book of Helaman (16:25), which Mormon wrote from the record of Helaman and his sons."]},
"4ne.1":{title:"There could not be a happier people",year:"About AD 35–321",
 sum:"The people are converted and have all things common, with no rich or poor and no contention—The three disciples and others work great miracles, and peace lasts nearly two hundred years—Pride, classes, and false churches begin to divide the people, and some take the name Lamanites again—Gadianton’s secret oaths return, and Ammaron hides up the sacred records.",
 bg:["Verses 2–3 and 15–17 describe a people who “had all things common” and lived “in one, the children of Christ” without any “manner of -ites.” This is similar to the early Church at Jerusalem (Acts 2:44–45; 4:32–34) and to Enoch’s city, where there was “no poor among them” (Moses 7:18). It is a rare example in scripture of a whole society living in unity over generations.","The peace ends slowly, over about a century, as people begin to wear “costly apparel” (1:24), form classes (1:26), and build churches “to get gain” (1:26–27). By verse 38 the old names of Lamanites, Lemuelites, and Ishmaelites return. In verses 47–49 the record passes to Amos and then Ammaron, who hides the plates, setting up Mormon’s account in the next book (Mormon 1:2–4)."]},
"morm.1":{title:"Tasted and knew of the goodness of Jesus",year:"About AD 322–326",
 sum:"Mormon begins his record, which he calls the Book of Mormon—Ammaron tells the ten-year-old Mormon to take the plates of Nephi when he is twenty-four—Mormon is taken to Zarahemla, where wars break out—The Lord takes away the disciples and gifts, and Mormon at fifteen is visited by the Lord but forbidden to preach.",
 bg:["Mormon’s name comes from the land of Mormon, where Alma baptized (Mosiah 18:4; 3 Nephi 5:12). A traditional Latter-day Saint explanation, attributed to Joseph Smith, is that “Mormon” means “more good,” but the Book of Mormon itself does not give a meaning. Mormon’s father was also named Mormon (1:5).","The record Ammaron hid was kept at a hill called Shim in the land Antum, and Mormon was told to go there when he was twenty-four (1:3). Mormon says he was “sober” and “quick to observe” as a child (1:2), and at fifteen he “tasted and knew of the goodness of Jesus” (1:15). The Lord told him to remember what he saw, though he could not preach (1:16–17)."]},
"morm.2":{title:"The sorrowing of the damned",year:"About AD 327–350",
 sum:"At sixteen Mormon leads the Nephite armies, and they retreat before the Lamanites—The people mourn, but not unto repentance, and the day of grace is past—Mormon takes the plates of Nephi from Shim and keeps a full account of their wickedness—A treaty in AD 350 divides the land.",
 bg:["Mormon says he was “large in stature” and was chosen to lead the armies when he was about fifteen or sixteen (2:1–2). The sorrow he sees is not a change of heart: it is “the sorrowing of the damned” (2:13), where people regret the loss, not the sin. Paul draws the same line in 2 Corinthians 7:10, between godly sorrow that leads to repentance and the sorrow of the world.","When the Nephites lose their goods, Mormon says it fulfills Samuel the Lamanite’s warning that no man would be able to keep what was his own (2:10; Helaman 13:34–36). In 2:17–18 Mormon explains that the longer record is on the plates of Nephi and these are a shorter abridgment, because a “continual scene of wickedness” was too much to write twice."]},
"morm.3":{title:"Vengeance is mine, and I will repay",year:"About AD 360–362",
 sum:"After ten years of peace, the Lord calls the people to repent and be baptized, but they harden their hearts—They win a battle and swear to avenge their brethren—Mormon refuses to lead them, and the Lord forbids them to go up to battle—Mormon writes to the Gentiles and the house of Israel, saying all will stand before the judgment-seat of Christ.",
 bg:["The Lord’s command in verse 15, “Vengeance is mine, and I will repay,” is a quotation from Deuteronomy 32:35, which Paul also quoted in Romans 12:19. The Nephites had sworn to avenge themselves, and the Lord’s answer is that revenge is not theirs to take. Mormon, who had led them many times, refuses to command them and stands as “an idle witness” (3:11–16).","From verse 17 Mormon speaks directly to readers in the last days: the Gentiles, the house of Israel, and the remnant of his own people. He says Jesus’s twelve disciples in Jerusalem and the twelve in the Americas will judge them (3:18–19), which echoes Matthew 19:28. In verses 20–21 he says the Jews will have “other witness” of Jesus, in line with the promise about the Bible and the Book of Mormon in 2 Nephi 29."]},
"morm.4":{title:"By the wicked that the wicked are punished",year:"About AD 363–375",
 sum:"The Nephites go to war against the Lamanites in AD 363 but are driven back—Mormon says the wicked stir up the hearts of men to bloodshed—Both peoples practice horrible cruelty, including human sacrifice, and the Nephites are swept off like dew before the sun—Mormon takes the plates from the hill Shim.",
 bg:["Mormon sees the Nephites defeated because they went up in anger to take revenge and not because the Lord was with them (4:4). His comment, “it is by the wicked that the wicked are punished” (4:5), is a hard saying that does not excuse either side. It is a reminder that violence tends to grow more violence.","This chapter describes terrible events, including the sacrifice of captured women and children to idols (4:14, 21), and Mormon says it is “impossible for the tongue to describe” them (4:11). He says that the Nephites’ wickedness was greater than any among “all the house of Israel” (4:12). Mormon recovers the hidden records at the hill Shim, as Ammaron had instructed (4:23)."]},
"morm.5":{title:"Clasped in the arms of Jesus",year:"About AD 375–384",
 sum:"Mormon agrees to lead again, though without hope—The Nephites retreat and lands are lost until AD 380—Mormon writes to the remnant, the Jews, and the Gentiles so they may know what happened—The Lord will remember the covenant with Abraham, and the Gentiles must repent lest the remnant go among them “as a lion.”",
 bg:["Mormon says the people “repented not” and did not call on God (5:2), yet he writes not to condemn them but so “that they might have been clasped in the arms of Jesus” (5:11). His purpose is clear in verses 8–14: the record is meant for “the remnant of the house of Jacob,” the unbelieving Jews, and the Gentiles. He also says the people had once been “a delightsome people” and had Christ for their shepherd (5:17).","In verses 20–24 Mormon says the Lord will remember his covenant to Abraham (see Genesis 12:2–3; 17:7), and warns that the remnant will “go forth among you as a lion,” which echoes Micah 5:8 and Christ’s own words in 3 Nephi 20:16. “The earth shall be rolled together as a scroll” in verse 23 uses an image from Isaiah 34:4 and Revelation 6:14."]},
"morm.6":{title:"O ye fair ones",year:"About AD 385",
 sum:"Mormon asks the Lamanite king for a battle at Cumorah, and the Nephites gather there—Mormon hides up the records in the hill Cumorah and gives a few plates to his son Moroni—The Nephite armies are cut down, and only twenty-four survive—Mormon laments for his people and says they will stand before the judgment-seat of Christ.",
 bg:["The meaning of the name Cumorah is not given in the text. Joseph Smith and many Church leaders have taught that the hill in New York where Joseph found the plates is Cumorah, while the Church takes no official position on where other Book of Mormon events took place, and some members hold different views. Mormon says it was a land “of many waters, rivers, and fountains” (6:4), and Ether 15:11 says the Jaredites called the same hill Ramah.","Mormon’s lament, “O ye fair ones, how could ye have departed from the ways of the Lord” and “how could ye have rejected that Jesus, who stood with open arms to receive you” (6:17), sounds like Jesus’s grief over Jerusalem in Matthew 23:37. Mormon also tells us that the army’s ten thousand soldiers were led by captains such as Gidgiddonah (6:13), a name like Gidgiddoni, the chief captain of 3 Nephi 3:18 who lived centuries earlier."]},
"morm.7":{title:"Know ye that ye are of the house of Israel",year:"About AD 385",
 sum:"Mormon speaks to the remnant of his people who will survive—Know that you are of the house of Israel, you must repent, believe in Jesus Christ, and be baptized—This record and the Bible should be read together, for each helps us believe the other—Mormon closes with his farewell.",
 bg:["The words “Know ye” run through these verses and call the remnant to remember who they are: they are “numbered among the people of the first covenant” and “a remnant of the seed of Jacob” (7:2, 10). Mormon sets out simple steps, which are to repent, believe in Christ, and be baptized with water and then with “fire and with the Holy Ghost” (7:3–10). This matches John 3:5 and Matthew 3:11.","Verses 8–9 say this record and “the record which shall come unto the Gentiles from the Jews” are written so that people will believe both. In Latter-day Saint teaching this refers to the Book of Mormon and the Bible, the two sticks in Ezekiel 37:15–20. Mormon’s own last words in verse 10 are a blessing for all who believe."]},
"morm.8":{title:"I even remain alone",year:"About AD 400–421",
 sum:"Moroni finishes his father’s record after the destruction at Cumorah, in which Mormon was killed—He says he is alone and will hide up the plates—He speaks of the record coming forth out of the earth and warns of pollutions in the last days—Moroni sees our day and cries against pride, fine apparel, and neglect of the poor.",
 bg:["Moroni writes, “I even remain alone” (8:3), as Elijah said, “I, even I only, am left” (1 Kings 19:10), after the Lamanites hunt down the remaining Nephites. He says four hundred years have passed since Christ came (8:6), and he does not know whether he will be killed (8:3–4). Verse 12 asks readers not to condemn the record “because of the imperfections which are in it,” which is a humble plea from someone who knew his record was flawed.","Verses 23–26 say the record will come “out of the earth” (verse 26) and that the saints will “cry, yea, even from the dust” (verse 23). This echoes Isaiah 29:4, where the people speak “out of the ground,” and Nephi’s similar words in 2 Nephi 26:15–16. In verses 35–41 Moroni says the Lord showed him our day, and he speaks to us about pride and neglect of the poor, as in Matthew 25:35–40."]},
"morm.9":{title:"Doubt not, but be believing",year:"About AD 400–421",
 sum:"Moroni asks those who deny Christ what they will say on the day of judgment—God is a God of miracles, the same yesterday, today, and forever—Miracles cease only because of unbelief—Christ’s promise to the believing is repeated, and Moroni asks readers not to condemn the record for its imperfections.",
 bg:["Moroni quotes Jesus’s words in verses 22–25: “Go ye into all the world, and preach the gospel to every creature,” and the signs that follow believers, which are nearly the same as Mark 16:15–18. Verse 25 adds a promise that the Lord will confirm “all my words, even unto the ends of the earth” to those who believe, “doubting nothing.” Many Bible scholars note that Mark 16:9–20 is missing from some of the earliest manuscripts, but Moroni treats these as words spoken by the risen Lord.","Moroni says God “is the same yesterday, today, and forever” (9:9), which uses the language of Hebrews 13:8 and Malachi 3:6. In verses 32–34 he says the record was written in “reformed Egyptian,” and that the Hebrew had been “altered by us also,” so “none other people knoweth our language.” The Lord has prepared a way to translate it (see also Mosiah 28:13–16). The people he asks not to condemn him (9:31) echo the title page, which says that any faults “are the mistakes of men.”"]},
"3ne.1":{title:"On this night shall the sign be given",year:"About AD 1",
 sum:"Believers and unbelievers watch for the sign Samuel the Lamanite foretold—Nephi prays and the Lord promises, “on the morrow come I into the world”—A night with no darkness and a new star mark the birth of Christ—Satan stirs up unbelief while the Gadianton robbers grow strong.",
 bg:["Samuel the Lamanite had given these signs five years earlier (Helaman 14:2–6). The “day and a night and a day” of light (1:15, 19) matches Helaman 14:4. Nephi here is the son of Nephi and grandson of Helaman, who handed the records to him (1:2).","The Lord’s voice to Nephi (1:13–14) says the Savior would come “fulfil all that which I have caused to be spoken by the mouth of my holy prophets,” echoing the language of promised prophecy in Isaiah 9:6 and Luke 2. The text sets the unbelievers’ doubts (1:22) beside the faithful, so the story shows the same sign received two different ways."]},
"3ne.2":{title:"The people began to forget those signs and wonders",year:"About AD 1–16",
 sum:"The people forget the signs and grow hard of heart—A new calendar begins from the sign of Christ’s coming—Gadianton robbers multiply until the Nephites are nearly destroyed—Converted Lamanites unite with the Nephites—By the sixteenth year the sword of destruction hangs over the people.",
 bg:["Verse 8 marks a change in how the Nephites count time: the years now run “from the coming of Christ.” Mormon uses this reckoning through the rest of the book, which is why events in 3 Nephi can be dated so precisely.","Verses 14–16 say the Lamanites who joined the Nephites had “their curse” removed and became “white.” The text does not explain the mechanism, and readers have long discussed it; elsewhere the Book of Mormon teaches that God’s invitation is to all, “black and white, bond and free” (2 Nephi 26:33)."]},
"3ne.3":{title:"Lachoneus, the governor, was a just man",year:"About AD 16–18",
 sum:"Giddianhi, leader of the Gadianton robbers, demands that the Nephites surrender—Lachoneus refuses and calls on the people to repent—Gidgiddoni, a prophet, is appointed chief captain—All the Nephites gather with their food and animals into one defended land.",
 bg:["The names Lachoneus, Giddianhi and Gidgiddoni do not have well-established meanings; no source I can verify gives one. Giddianhi’s letter (3:2–10) is full of flattery and threats, a pattern of intimidation still easy to recognize today.","Gidgiddoni refuses to attack first (3:20–21), saying the Lord “would deliver us into their hands” if the Nephites did. This echoes earlier Nephite teaching that defense, not aggression, was their cause (see Alma 43:45–47). The gathering place lay between Zarahemla and Bountiful (3:23)."]},
"3ne.4":{title:"Hosanna to the Most High God",year:"About AD 18–19",
 sum:"The robbers come down on the Nephites but cannot find food in the empty lands—Giddianhi attacks in the nineteenth year and is defeated and slain—Zemnarihah, the new leader, is besieged and later hanged on a tree—The Nephites thank God and sing praises.",
 bg:["Nephite strategy worked by hunger more than by sword: the lands they left behind had “no game,” so the robbers had to fight or starve (4:2–4). The text credits the Nephites’ prayers and their obedience to Gidgiddoni’s counsel (4:10).","“Hosanna” comes from Hebrew words meaning something like “save now” (Psalm 118:25). The people sing it here, and the crowd cries it again when they see Jesus in 3 Nephi 11:17. The meaning of the name Zemnarihah is not known."]},
"3ne.5":{title:"I am called Mormon",year:"About AD 20–26",
 sum:"The people forsake their sins and the robbers are converted or destroyed—Twenty-five years pass in peace—Mormon introduces himself and his work as abridger—He gives thanks that the Lord brought Lehi out of Jerusalem and promises the gathering of Israel.",
 bg:["Verses 12–13 are the first time the abridger speaks in his own name. He says he was named for the land of Mormon, where Alma baptized and began the first church (Mosiah 18:4–17). Scholars do not agree on what the name “Mormon” originally meant.","Mormon is a “pure descendant of Lehi” (5:20) who says he wrote from the plates of Nephi and from records of “those who were before me” (compare 5:9–19). His closing hope (5:21–26) speaks of the gathering of Israel “from the four quarters of the earth,” language that matches Isaiah 11:12."]},
"3ne.6":{title:"There became a great inequality in all the land",year:"About AD 26–30",
 sum:"The Nephites return to their lands and rebuild with roads and cities in great peace—Pride, riches and class divisions slowly break up the church—Prophets are secretly put to death by judges—Lawyers, judges and high priests join a secret combination against the governor.",
 bg:["Mormon names the causes of the fall plainly: merchants, lawyers and officers; ranks “according to their riches and their chances for learning” (6:11–12). The scripture does not say wealth was evil. It says pride and inequality “broke up” the church (6:14).","The judges who killed the prophets acted “contrary to the laws of the land,” since only the governor could order an execution (6:22–24). This is similar to Pilate’s authority in John 19:10, where only Rome could put a man to death."]},
"3ne.7":{title:"They separated one from another into tribes",year:"About AD 30–33",
 sum:"The government is destroyed and the people divide into tribes led by their own chiefs—Jacob, head of a secret band, is made king and flees to the north—Nephi testifies boldly, casts out devils and raises his brother from the dead—Few believe, but those who are converted are baptized.",
 bg:["The name Jacob usually means “heel-holder” or “supplanter” (Genesis 25:26; 27:36). The irony here is sharp: a man with the name of Israel’s ancestor leads a band that kills prophets and is later called the “king” of a wicked tribe (7:9–10).","Nephi’s brother Timothy is raised from the dead “after he had been stoned” (7:19), and Mormon says even this could not move most of the people (7:18–22). The Nephites are divided into tribes (7:2–4), which echoes the way their ancient Israelite ancestors were organized before the monarchy."]},
"3ne.8":{title:"O that we had repented before this great and terrible day",year:"About AD 34",
 sum:"A great storm, earthquakes and fire begin in the first month of the thirty-fourth year—Cities are burned, sunk and buried, with greater ruin in the land northward—Thick darkness covers the land for three days—The survivors mourn and cry that they had not repented.",
 bg:["This is the fulfilment of Samuel the Lamanite’s prophecy of three days’ darkness and great upheaval at the death of Christ (Helaman 14:20–27). Matthew 27:45, 51–54 also records darkness and an earthquake at the crucifixion, though the New Testament mentions only a part of a day for the darkness.","The “thick darkness” (8:20–23) echoes the ninth plague in Exodus 10:21–23, which was also three days long. Mormon says his own record may have a mistake in the reckoning of time (8:2), a frank note from an abridger who is working from older plates."]},
"3ne.9":{title:"I am the light and the life of the world",year:"About AD 34",
 sum:"A voice from the darkness tells which cities were destroyed and why—The Lord invites the survivors to return to him and be healed—He declares himself Jesus Christ, the Creator, who came to his own and was rejected—Sacrifice by blood is ended and a broken heart and contrite spirit are asked in its place.",
 bg:["The cities named include several named after Gadianton robbers or their leaders, such as Kishkumen (9:10) and Jacobugath (9:9, “inhabited by the people of king Jacob”; see 3 Nephi 7). Many of these names appear nowhere else in the Book of Mormon.","Verses 15–18 echo John 1:1–12 and Revelation 22:13 (“Alpha and Omega”). Verses 19–20 end the law of sacrifice and ask for “a broken heart and a contrite spirit,” language that also appears in Psalm 51:17 and 2 Nephi 2:7."]},
"3ne.10":{title:"As a hen gathereth her chickens under her wings",year:"About AD 34",
 sum:"The people fall silent for many hours after the voice—The Lord speaks again and says he has often wanted to gather them as a hen gathers her chickens—The darkness lifts after three days and the earth is still—Mormon says Zenos, Zenock and others foretold these things.",
 bg:["The hen and chickens image is also spoken in Matthew 23:37 and Luke 13:34, where Jesus mourned over Jerusalem. Here he speaks it to the survivors in Nephite lands and adds, “if ye will repent and return unto me with full purpose of heart” (10:6). The same image appears in Doctrine and Covenants 29:2 and 43:24.","Mormon mentions Zenos (10:16; Jacob 5 gives his allegory of the olive tree) and Zenock (10:16; Alma 33:15–17 quotes him). These are prophets from the brass plates that we do not have in the Bible, which shows how much ancient scripture the Nephites had."]},
"3ne.11":{title:"Behold my Beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased",year:"About AD 34–35",
 sum:"The people hear the Father’s voice, a small voice that pierces to the soul, and understand it on the third time—The Father introduces his Son and Jesus descends and declares himself—The multitude feel the marks of his wounds—Jesus gives authority to baptize and teaches his doctrine of faith, repentance and baptism.",
 bg:["The Father says, “Behold my Beloved Son … hear ye him” (11:7). Matthew 3:17 and 17:5 record the Father introducing his Son at the Jordan and on the mount, and Joseph Smith’s First Vision records the same words in Joseph Smith—History 1:17. The “still small voice” echoes Elijah’s experience in 1 Kings 19:12.","Verses 23–26 give the baptismal prayer and say candidates must be “immersed.” This closely parallels Doctrine and Covenants 20:73. The Greek word baptizo used in the New Testament means “dip” or “immerse.” “Hosanna” in verse 17 is from Hebrew “save now” (Psalm 118:25)."]},
"3ne.12":{title:"Old things are done away, and all things have become new",year:"About AD 34–35",
 sum:"Jesus calls twelve disciples and promises a baptism of fire and the Holy Ghost—He gives the beatitudes, sets his disciples as the light of the people, and says the law of Moses is fulfilled in him—He teaches higher laws on anger, purity, oaths, retaliation and loving enemies—He ends with the call to be perfect.",
 bg:["This is the Sermon on the Mount (Matthew 5). The Book of Mormon version begins differently: verses 1–2 speak of baptism and of the Holy Ghost first, then the beatitudes begin. Verse 3 reads “the poor in spirit who come unto me,” verse 6 says they “shall be filled with the Holy Ghost,” and verse 10 says “for my name’s sake.” Verse 22 leaves out the phrase “without a cause” found in the KJV of Matthew 5:22.","In Matthew 5:17–18 Jesus says he came to fulfil the law. Here he adds that the law “hath been fulfilled” in him and “old things are done away” (12:18–19, 46–47). Verse 48 is also different: “Therefore I would that ye should be perfect even as I, or your Father who is in heaven is perfect,” while Matthew 5:48 says “be ye therefore perfect, even as your Father which is in heaven is perfect.” The Greek word for “perfect” in Matthew means “complete” or “whole.” Jesus later uses the same words in 3 Nephi 27:27."]},
"3ne.13":{title:"After this manner therefore pray ye",year:"About AD 34–35",
 sum:"Jesus teaches that alms, prayer and fasting should be done in secret—He gives the model prayer—He urges treasure in heaven and an eye single to God—To the twelve he teaches no thought for what to eat, drink or wear.",
 bg:["This chapter follows Matthew 6 closely. The Lord’s Prayer is in verses 9–13, almost word for word with Matthew 6:9–13, including “debts” and “debtors” (Greek opheilemata and opheiletais, money owed). Luke 11:4 says “sins.” The final line, “for thine is the kingdom, and the power, and the glory,” is in the KJV of Matthew 6:13 but missing from many of the earliest Greek manuscripts.","Verses 25–34 are spoken to the twelve, not the whole crowd (13:25). In Matthew the same teaching is for the disciples in the wider setting. “Mammon” (13:24) is an Aramaic word for money or property. The Book of Mormon text of this chapter is very close to the KJV."]},
"3ne.14":{title:"Ask, and it shall be given unto you",year:"About AD 34–35",
 sum:"Jesus turns again to the multitude and warns them not to judge—He teaches that prayer is answered—He gives the golden rule and the strait gate—He warns of false prophets and teaches that those who do the Father’s will build upon a rock.",
 bg:["This chapter follows Matthew 7 almost word for word, from “Judge not” to the wise and foolish builders. The text notes that Jesus “turned again to the multitude” (14:1), which shows he spoke to the twelve at the end of chapter 13.","Verse 12 is the golden rule, which Jesus says is “the law and the prophets,” the same summary of the Old Testament he gave in Matthew 7:12. The two roads (14:13–14) and the two builders (14:24–27) are teaching pictures about choices."]},
"3ne.15":{title:"I am the law, and the light",year:"About AD 34–35",
 sum:"Jesus says that the law of Moses is fulfilled in him—He explains that “old things” passing away did not mean the covenant with Israel was ended—He tells the Nephites they are the “other sheep” he spoke of at Jerusalem—He says the other tribes of Israel are also to hear his voice.",
 bg:["Verses 2–10 explain 3 Nephi 12:46–47. Jesus says he “gave the law” to Moses (15:5), and that the law is fulfilled, not the covenant: “the covenant which I have made with my people is not all fulfilled” (15:8). This answers a question many Latter-day Saints have about how the Old Testament law continues.","Verse 17 quotes John 10:16, “other sheep I have, which are not of this fold.” Jesus says the Jews at Jerusalem “understood not” and supposed it meant the Gentiles (15:18–24; see 16:1–3). The Book of Mormon claims the Nephites, and the “other tribes” (15:15, 19–20), are among them."]},
"3ne.16":{title:"I have other sheep, which are not of this land",year:"About AD 34–35",
 sum:"Jesus says he has still other sheep who have not yet heard his voice—He prophesies that the Gentiles will receive the gospel and then reject it—The gospel will go to the house of Israel, who will be gathered—He quotes Isaiah’s words about watchmen, joy and the holy arm of the Lord.",
 bg:["Verses 18–20 quote Isaiah 52:8–10: “Thy watchmen shall lift up the voice … they shall see eye to eye,” “break forth into joy,” and “the Lord hath made bare his holy arm.” The wording is close to the KJV. Abinadi quotes Isaiah 52:7–10 in Mosiah 12:21–24. Isaiah’s image: sentries on the walls see the Lord coming home to Zion and shout the news, and “bare” the arm means a warrior rolling up his sleeve to act.","Verses 8–15 describe the Gentiles in the latter days. Verses 14–15 use the image from Micah 5:8 of a “young lion among the flocks of sheep” and “salt that hath lost its savor” (compare Matthew 5:13). Verse 16 repeats that this land is given to the remnant of Jacob as an inheritance."]},
"3ne.17":{title:"Behold your little ones",year:"About AD 34–35",
 sum:"Jesus sees that the people are weak and tells them to go home and ponder—Moved by their tears, he stays and heals all the sick—He prays for the people in words too wonderful to write—He weeps, blesses the children one by one, and angels come down in fire.",
 bg:["Verse 6 says his “bowels” are filled with compassion. In the KJV style, “bowels” means the deep seat of feeling, like “heart” today. The word in Greek for the same idea in the New Testament is splagchnizomai, used when Jesus had compassion in Matthew 9:36 and 14:14.","The scene with the children (17:11–25) is the only place in scripture that describes angels encircling little ones “as it were in the midst of fire.” Compare Mark 10:13–16, where Jesus lays hands on children and blesses them. The writer says that “no tongue can speak” the joy of those who saw and heard (17:16–17)."]},
"3ne.18":{title:"This shall ye do in remembrance of my body",year:"About AD 34–35",
 sum:"Jesus gives the disciples bread and wine and teaches them the sacrament—He commands them to watch and pray always, pray in their families, and hold up his light—He teaches that the unworthy should not partake knowingly but should be ministered to—He gives the twelve power to give the Holy Ghost and ascends.",
 bg:["This sacrament is given in the Book of Mormon “in remembrance of my body” and “my blood” (18:7, 11) and to witness “that ye do always remember me.” That wording is close to the Last Supper in Matthew 26:26–28 and 1 Corinthians 11:23–26. The warning about unworthy partaking in verses 28–29 follows 1 Corinthians 11:27–29. Doctrine and Covenants 27:2 later teaches that the Lord cares more about remembering him than what is used.","Verses 24–25 repeat “hold up your light … I am the light,” and verse 18 uses “sift you as wheat,” the image from Luke 22:31. The bread and wine were brought by the disciples (18:3); in chapter 20 Jesus provides them by miracle."]},
"3ne.19":{title:"They did pray for that which they most desired",year:"About AD 34–35",
 sum:"The multitude gathers again on the next day and the twelve are named—The disciples pray for the Holy Ghost, are baptized, and are encircled with fire and angels—Jesus prays and the disciples are made white—He prays that the Father give the Holy Ghost and make them one—He says he has never seen such faith among the Jews.",
 bg:["The disciples’ names (19:4) are Nephi, Timothy, Jonas, Mathoni, Mathonihah, Kumen, Kumenonhi, Jeremiah, Shemnon, Jonas, Zedekiah and Isaiah. Timothy is Greek and often explained as “honoring God”; Jonas is the Greek form of Jonah, “dove”; Zedekiah means “righteousness of Yahweh” and Isaiah “Yahweh saves.” The origin of Mathoni, Mathonihah, Kumen and Kumenonhi is uncertain.","Jesus’ prayer in 19:20–29 recalls the great prayer of John 17, where he also asks that his disciples be “one” (John 17:21–23). The crowd’s chief desire was the Holy Ghost (19:9). The text says their countenance and garments became as white as Jesus’ own (19:25), and that “so great faith have I never seen among all the Jews” (19:35)."]},
"3ne.20":{title:"He truly gave unto them bread to eat",year:"About AD 34–35",
 sum:"Jesus provides bread and wine by miracle and the people are filled with the Spirit—He promises the Father will gather Israel and that the remnant of Jacob will be a lion among the Gentiles—He says this land is the inheritance and the site of the New Jerusalem—He quotes Isaiah 52 on Zion awaking and the beautiful feet of the messenger.",
 bg:["Verses 16–19 echo Micah 5:8–9, where the remnant of Jacob among the nations is “as a lion among the beasts of the forest.” Verse 23 identifies Jesus as the “prophet … like unto me” promised in Deuteronomy 18:15–19; compare Acts 3:22–23.","Verses 32–45 contain Isaiah 52:1–3, 6–15 in the form of Jesus’ own prophecy. Abinadi quoted Isaiah 52:7–10 in Mosiah 12:21–24. Isaiah’s poem calls Zion to wake, shake off the dust, and put on beautiful garments, a picture of a city dressing for a return from exile. The “servant” of verse 43–44 who is “marred” echoes Isaiah 52:14, where the same word appears."]},
"3ne.21":{title:"A great and a marvelous work",year:"About AD 34–35",
 sum:"Jesus gives a sign that the gathering of Israel is near—He says the Father will do “a great and a marvelous work” among the Gentiles—He warns of the Gentiles who reject the gospel and describes the remnant as a lion among sheep—If the Gentiles repent they will join the covenant people and help build the New Jerusalem.",
 bg:["Verses 12–21 follow Micah 5:8–15, with Jesus describing the remnant as a lion and promising that horses, chariots, cities, witchcraft and graven images will be cut off. The phrase “a marvelous work” (21:9) matches the language of Isaiah 29:14 that Nephi also quoted in 2 Nephi 25:17; 27:26. The text does not say how the “servant” of verses 10–11 should be identified, and readers have understood it in more than one way.","In verses 22–25 Jesus says that Gentiles who repent “shall assist my people” to “build a city, which shall be called the New Jerusalem” (21:23). Ether 13:3–6 also speaks of the New Jerusalem on this land."]},
"3ne.22":{title:"Enlarge the place of thy tent",year:"About AD 34–35",
 sum:"Jesus quotes Isaiah’s call to the barren woman to sing—Zion is told to enlarge her tents and not to fear—The Lord who is her Maker and husband will gather her with mercy—Her children will be taught of the Lord and no weapon formed against her will prosper.",
 bg:["This chapter is Isaiah 54, which Jesus tells the Nephites to search (23:1–3). The wording is nearly the same as the KJV, with a few small differences such as verse 15, which reads “shall surely gather together against thee, not by me.” Isaiah’s poem imagines Israel as a childless wife who is promised more children than a married woman, and the “tent” and “cords” are an image of a nomad’s home that must be made larger.","Verse 9 mentions “the waters of Noah” as a promise from the Lord never to flood the earth again, and verses 11–12 describe a city built with sapphires, agates and jewels. Verse 17 is the passage “no weapon that is formed against thee shall prosper.” Verse 13 says all her children “shall be taught of the Lord,” which Jesus uses in John 6:45."]},
"3ne.23":{title:"Search these things diligently",year:"About AD 34–35",
 sum:"Jesus commands them to search Isaiah, because his words reach Israel and the Gentiles—He tells them to search all the prophets—He asks to see the Nephite record and notes that the fulfilment of Samuel the Lamanite’s prophecy about the saints arising was not written—Nephi writes it.",
 bg:["“Great are the words of Isaiah” (23:1) is the Savior’s own endorsement of Isaiah and is why the Book of Mormon quotes him so much (see 1 Nephi 19:23; 2 Nephi 25:1–8). Compare John 5:39, “Search the scriptures.”","Verses 9–13 mention that many saints arose at Jesus’ resurrection and ministered to many, as Samuel the Lamanite had said (Helaman 14:25). Matthew 27:52–53 reports the same event in Jerusalem. The omission shows even prophets can forget details until the Lord reminds them."]},
"3ne.24":{title:"I will send my messenger",year:"About AD 34–35",
 sum:"Jesus has the people write Malachi’s words that the Father gave him—The Lord will send his messenger and then come to his temple—He will be like a refiner’s fire and the Levites will be purified—The people are called to return, pay tithes and offerings, and a book of remembrance is kept.",
 bg:["This chapter is Malachi 3. The Hebrew name Malachi, “my messenger,” is the same word used in the first verse: “I will send my messenger.” Matthew 11:10 applies this verse to John the Baptist, and the verse also points ahead to the Lord’s own coming to his temple. The wording follows the KJV closely, with a few small differences.","Verses 8–12 are the famous passage on tithes and offerings: “Will a man rob God?” and the promise to “open you the windows of heaven.” Verse 16 speaks of a “book of remembrance” and verse 17 calls the faithful the Lord’s “jewels.” Malachi is usually dated by scholars to about the fifth century BC, though the exact date is not known."]},
"3ne.25":{title:"The Son of Righteousness shall arise with healing in his wings",year:"About AD 34–35",
 sum:"The day of the Lord burns like an oven and consumes the proud—For those who fear his name the “Son of Righteousness” arises with healing—They are told to remember the law of Moses—The Lord will send Elijah to turn the hearts of the fathers and the children to each other.",
 bg:["This chapter is Malachi 4. Verse 2 reads “Son of Righteousness,” where the KJV has “Sun of righteousness,” and says “calves in the stall,” where the KJV has “calves of the stall.” Latter-day Saints read the verse as pointing to Christ and sometimes note the play on “Sun” and “Son.”","Verses 5–6 promise Elijah. Moroni quoted the same passage to Joseph Smith with changes (Joseph Smith—History 1:36–39; Doctrine and Covenants 2). Elijah appeared in the Kirtland Temple on 3 April 1836 (Doctrine and Covenants 110:13–16). Malachi 4:6 ends the Old Testament, and the verse is at the root of Latter-day Saint family history work."]},
"3ne.26":{title:"Babes did open their mouths and utter marvelous things",year:"About AD 34–35",
 sum:"Jesus expounds all things from the beginning to the end of the world—Mormon says he is forbidden to write the greater things so that faith may be tried—Children speak marvelous things—Those who are baptized form the church of Christ and live in unity.",
 bg:["Verse 3 reaches as far as “the elements should melt with fervent heat” and the earth be “wrapt together as a scroll.” These images recall 2 Peter 3:10 and Isaiah 34:4. Mormon is clear that “cannot be written in this book even a hundredth part” of what Jesus taught (26:6).","Verses 9–11 say a greater record is withheld to try people’s faith, matching Alma 12:9–11 and Ether 4:6–7. In verse 16, the babes who speak marvelous things recall Psalm 8:2 and Matthew 21:16. Verse 19 says they “had all things common” and lived justly, an echo of Acts 4:32, with a result 4 Nephi 1:3 describes as well."]},
"3ne.27":{title:"What manner of men ought ye to be",year:"About AD 34–35",
 sum:"Jesus tells the disciples to call the church by his name—He defines his gospel: the Father sent him to be lifted up on the cross to draw all people to him—Those who repent, are baptized and endure are sanctified by the Holy Ghost—He asks, “What manner of men ought ye to be?” and answers, “Even as I am.”",
 bg:["Verses 5–8 give a reason for the name of the church: a church named for Moses would be Moses’ church, one named for a man would be that man’s church, but one named for Christ is his. In our time the Lord said the church would be called “The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints” (Doctrine and Covenants 115:4).","Verses 13–22 give “my gospel” in full: the Atonement, repentance, baptism, and the Holy Ghost. The phrase “draw all men unto me” echoes John 12:32. Verse 27’s “even as I am” sits beside 3 Nephi 12:48, so the invitation to “be perfect” is explained as becoming like Christ."]},
"3ne.28":{title:"What is it that ye desire of me",year:"About AD 34–35",
 sum:"Jesus asks each disciple what he desires—Nine ask to come to him at age seventy-two and are promised rest—Three desire to stay on earth and bring souls to Christ until he returns and are promised they will not taste death—They are changed to a translated state, minister to the world, and are not harmed.",
 bg:["The three desired the same thing as John, “my beloved” (28:6). John 21:20–23 says that some thought John would not die, and Doctrine and Covenants 7:1–3 says the Lord granted John’s desire to “tarry” until his return. The text does not name the three.","Mormon says he could not tell whether the three were “in the body or out of the body” when they were changed (28:15). Paul used almost the same words about his own experience in 2 Corinthians 12:2–3. Mormon also says that he does not know whether they are mortal or immortal now (28:17), and that they will remain until the Lord returns (28:7–8, 36–40)."]},
"3ne.29":{title:"The Lord will remember his covenant",year:"About AD 385",
 sum:"Mormon says that when this record comes forth, the gathering of Israel has begun—Those who deny revelation, miracles and the Christ are warned—The Gentiles should not hiss at or mock the Jews—The Lord will fulfil his covenant with the house of Israel.",
 bg:["Mormon speaks directly to people of our day, a time he says “the Lord shall see fit” (29:1). The coming forth of this record is called a sign that the Father has “commenced” to fulfil his covenants to Israel. Verse 6 warns against those who say the Lord “no longer worketh by revelation, or by prophecy, or by gifts, or by tongues, or by healings,” a theme repeated in Mormon 9:7–8.","Verse 8 tells the Gentiles to stop mocking the Jews or other remnant of Israel, because the Lord “remembereth his covenant unto them.” Mormon finished the abridgment around AD 385, the time of the last great Nephite battles (Mormon 6), though the exact year is not stated."]},
"3ne.30":{title:"Turn, all ye Gentiles, from your wicked ways",year:"About AD 385",
 sum:"Mormon speaks to the Gentiles in the name of Jesus Christ—They are called to repent of lies, deceits, murders, idolatries and priestcrafts—They are invited to come unto Christ, be baptized, and receive the Holy Ghost—Those who do will be numbered with the house of Israel.",
 bg:["This short chapter closes Mormon’s direct message and ends the account of the Savior’s visit begun in chapter 11. The Gentiles here are non-Israelites who would receive the Book of Mormon; the promise is that those who repent will be “numbered with my people who are of the house of Israel” (30:2), as the Lord said in 3 Nephi 16:13 and 21:6, 22.","“Priestcrafts” (30:2) means teaching or leading for pay or praise instead of serving. Nephi defines it in 2 Nephi 26:29 as setting yourself up “for a light unto the world, that they may get gain and praise of the world.” The next book, 4 Nephi, picks up the story of the Nephites after Jesus’ visit."]},
"ether.1":{title:"Cry unto the Lord",year:"About 2200 BC",
 sum:"Moroni begins his abridgment of the Jaredite record from the twenty-four plates found by the people of Limhi—Ether’s ancestors are listed back to Jared—Jared and his brother cry to the Lord at the tower, and their language is not confounded—The Lord promises to lead them to a choice land and raise up a great nation.",
 bg:["Moroni says he takes his account “from the twenty and four plates which were found by the people of Limhi” (1:2; see Mosiah 8:7–9). He gives only part of the story, “from the tower down until they were destroyed” (1:5).","The tower is the one in Genesis 11. There the name Babel is tied to a Hebrew word meaning “to confuse,” and Genesis 11:9 says the Lord did “confound” the language. Ether 1:33–35 uses the same word. The name Jared matches a name in Genesis 5:15, which Hebrew scholars usually connect with “descent.” The Book of Mormon does not explain the name."]},
"ether.2":{title:"What will ye that I should do?",year:"About 2200 BC",
 sum:"Jared’s company goes down into the valley of Nimrod, carrying flocks, seeds and swarms of bees—The Lord leads them through the wilderness toward a promised land that must be served in righteousness—At the sea the brother of Jared is chastened and forgiven—He builds barges as directed and brings the problem of light to the Lord.",
 bg:["Verse 1 says the valley was “called after the mighty hunter,” the same Nimrod of Genesis 10:8–9. Verse 3 says the word deseret means “a honey bee.” The Lord promised a land “choice above all other lands” (2:7), and Moroni adds that its people must serve “the God of the land, who is Jesus Christ” (2:12).","The company camped by the sea at a place they called Moriancumer for four years (2:13). A Latter-day Saint tradition, not found in the text of the Book of Mormon, gives the brother of Jared’s name as Mahonri Moriancumer. In the barge story, notice that the Lord solves the air and steering problems Himself but asks the brother of Jared, “What will ye that I should do?” (2:23, 25)."]},
"ether.3":{title:"I saw the finger of the Lord",year:"About 2200 BC",
 sum:"The brother of Jared melts sixteen clear stones from the mount Shelem and asks the Lord to touch them with His finger—He sees the finger of the Lord and falls in fear—Because of his faith the Lord shows Himself: “Behold, I am Jesus Christ”—Man is created in the image of the Lord’s spirit body—He is told to seal up what he has seen, together with two stones for its later interpretation.",
 bg:["The Lord says, “Behold, this body, which ye now behold, is the body of my spirit; and man have I created after the body of my spirit” (3:16). This connects to Genesis 1:26–27, where man is made in God’s image. Moroni explains in verse 17 that Jesus appeared in the spirit, in the likeness of the body He would later take in the flesh.","The Lord told the brother of Jared to seal up his writings and two stones, which would one day help men read them (3:21–24, 28). Mosiah 28:11–17 describes King Mosiah translating the Jaredite record with “interpreters.” Moroni shows in Ether 12:20–21 why he thought this account was so important: it is a story of faith."]},
"ether.4":{title:"Rend that veil of unbelief",year:"About AD 400–421",
 sum:"Moroni is commanded to hide up the sealed record of the brother of Jared’s vision—It will not go to the Gentiles until they repent and have faith like the brother of Jared—Whatever persuades men to do good is of Christ—The Lord invites Gentile and house of Israel to come unto Him and rend the veil of unbelief—Those who believe and are baptized shall be saved.",
 bg:["These are Moroni’s words, quoting what the Lord told him. The sealed portion of the plates also appears in 2 Nephi 27:6–11, which describes a sealed book that will be revealed in the Lord’s own time. Verse 16 says the revelations “written by my servant John” would then be unfolded, which Latter-day Saints connect to the book of Revelation.","Verse 18, “he that believeth and is baptized shall be saved; but he that believeth not shall be damned,” closely echoes Mark 16:16. Mormon 9:22–25 also echoes the longer ending of Mark 16."]},
"ether.5":{title:"In the mouth of three witnesses",year:"About AD 400–421",
 sum:"Moroni tells the translator not to touch the sealed part—Three witnesses will be shown the plates by the power of God—In the mouth of three witnesses shall these things be established—Those who repent and come to the Father in Christ’s name will be received—Moroni will be a witness before God at the last day.",
 bg:["Moroni looks ahead to the Three Witnesses of the Book of Mormon. Their testimony is described in 2 Nephi 27:12–13. Doctrine and Covenants 17:1–5 records the promise that they would see the plates, and the Lord’s calling of them is found in Doctrine and Covenants 5:11–15.","Verse 4 uses wording from the law of Moses: “in the mouth of two or three witnesses” a matter is established (Deuteronomy 19:15; 2 Corinthians 13:1). In verse 6 Moroni invites readers to judge for themselves, saying, “ye shall know that I have authority when ye shall see me.”"]},
"ether.6":{title:"Driven forth before the wind",year:"About 2200 BC",
 sum:"The stones give light in the eight barges—The Lord sends a furious wind toward the promised land, and the people are buried many times in the deep but kept safe—They praise the Lord all day and night for three hundred forty-four days—They land, bow down and shed tears of joy—The people ask for a king, and Orihah is anointed after the other sons refuse.",
 bg:["Verse 7 compares the barges to “the ark of Noah” (Genesis 6–8). Both vessels were tight against the water and were driven by a wind the Lord controlled. Verse 12 says the people “did shed tears of joy before the Lord, because of the multitude of his tender mercies over them.”","The brother of Jared said of kingship, “Surely this thing leadeth into captivity” (6:23), a warning that sounds like Samuel’s words to Israel in 1 Samuel 8:11–18. Chapter 7:5 says this came true. The chapter names Jared’s four sons, Jacom, Gilgah, Mahah and Orihah (6:14), and the firstborn of the brother of Jared, Pagag, who refused to be king (6:25). The brother of Jared’s own name is never given in the Book of Mormon."]},
"ether.7":{title:"There were no more wars in the days of Shule",year:"Jaredite history, about 2000–600 BC",
 sum:"Orihah reigns righteously, and his son Kib has a son, Corihor, who rebels and takes his father captive—Shule, born in captivity, makes steel swords, defeats Corihor and restores the kingdom—Noah and Cohor rebel and the land is divided—Prophets warn of idolatry, and Shule protects them—The people repent and prosper.",
 bg:["Corihor’s rebellion fulfills the warning of the brother of Jared in 6:23 (see 7:5). The land of Moron, where Kib lived, was “near the land which is called Desolation by the Nephites” (7:6; compare Alma 22:30). The text gives no dates for these kings, which is why the chronology is described as uncertain.","Verse 9 says Shule made “swords out of steel.” The book does not explain what this metal was, and scholars disagree about it. Shule’s law that gave prophets freedom to go “whithersoever they would” (7:25) shows how righteous rule can protect the word of God."]},
"ether.8":{title:"The Lord worketh not in secret combinations",year:"Jaredite history, about 2000–600 BC",
 sum:"Jared rebels against his father Omer and takes the kingdom, but is later defeated by his brothers—His daughter proposes a plan to win it back, and Akish agrees to murder the king—Akish and his kindred swear secret oaths handed down from Cain—Moroni warns the Gentiles that nations that support secret combinations will be destroyed.",
 bg:["Verse 15 says the oaths came “from Cain, who was a murderer from the beginning,” and verse 16 says the devil kept them going. Compare Moses 5:29–31, where Cain makes a pact with Satan, and Helaman 6:26–30, which describes the same kind of oaths among the Gadianton robbers. Moroni chooses not to describe the oaths themselves (8:20).","Moroni says this evil destroyed both the Jaredites and the Nephites (8:21) and warns that it can arise in any nation (8:22–25). Moroni’s words are a plain warning rather than a political statement. The chapter says the Lord “worketh not in secret combinations” and has forbidden shedding blood “from the beginning” (8:19)."]},
"ether.9":{title:"Prepare the way of the Lord",year:"Jaredite history, about 2000–600 BC",
 sum:"The secret combination overthrows Omer, whom the Lord warns in a dream to flee—Akish murders his father-in-law, imprisons and starves his own son, and a long civil war leaves only thirty survivors—Omer is restored, and Emer’s righteous reign brings prosperity—Heth murders his father and prophets are rejected—Famine and serpents come until the people humble themselves.",
 bg:["Verse 19 lists “elephants and cureloms and cumoms.” The book does not describe the last two animals, so no one knows what they were. Verse 22 says Emer “saw the Son of Righteousness,” a phrase that recalls Malachi 4:2 (“Sun of righteousness”), which is quoted in 3 Nephi 25:2.","When the prophets cry that the people “must prepare the way of the Lord” (9:28), the words match Isaiah 40:3 and Matthew 3:3, about John the Baptist. The people threw prophets into pits (9:29), and a drought and poisonous serpents followed (9:30–31). When the people humbled themselves, the Lord “did send rain” (9:35)."]},
"ether.10":{title:"Never could be a people more blessed",year:"Jaredite history, about 2000–600 BC",
 sum:"Shez rebuilds a broken people, and his son Riplakish burdens them with heavy taxes and forced labor until he is killed—Morianton, Kim and Levi follow, with mixed righteousness—In the days of Lib the serpents are destroyed and the people build a great city by the narrow neck of land—The Jaredites prosper in all kinds of work—Robbers arise in the days of Com.",
 bg:["Riplakish took people’s labor and had prisoners do fine work in prison (10:5–7). His heavy burdens bring to mind the complaint against Rehoboam in 1 Kings 12:4. The “narrow neck of land” and “the sea divides the land” (10:20) are also named in Alma 22:32, and we do not know their location.","Verses 22–28 describe cities, metalwork, trade, tools and weapons, and say that “never could be a people more blessed than were they.” The chapter then shows how quickly this blessing could slip away. In the last verses of the chapter (10:30–33), kings live in captivity and robbers adopt “the old plans” of secret oaths."]},
"ether.11":{title:"The prophets were rejected by the people",year:"Jaredite history, about 2000–600 BC",
 sum:"In the days of Com many prophets warn of destruction unless the people repent, and Com is blessed—Shiblom’s brother rebels, kills the prophets and brings war, famine and pestilence—The people repent and the Lord has mercy, but Ahah and Ethem return to evil—Prophets again warn that another people will possess the land—Coriantor’s son Ether is born.",
 bg:["This chapter follows the family line given in Ether 1:6–32 in reverse, from Com through Shiblom, Seth, Ahah, Ethem, Moron and Coriantor down to Ether (11:4–23). The book gives no explanation for the names, and the dates are unknown. The Jaredite record often repeats names, such as Com, Heth, Coriantum and Lib, across different generations.","Verse 21 predicts that “the Lord God would send or bring forth another people to possess the land, by his power, after the manner by which he brought their fathers.” This is the pattern that repeats through Ether’s warning that choice land belongs to those who serve God (see 2:8–12). The prophets mourned and withdrew when the people hardened their hearts (11:13)."]},
"ether.12":{title:"Faith is things which are hoped for and not seen",year:"About AD 400–421",
 sum:"Ether prophesies, but the people will not believe what they cannot see—Moroni teaches that faith precedes miracles and lists the faithful from the brother of Jared to the Nephites—The Lord tells Moroni that “my grace is sufficient” and gives men weakness that they may be humble—Faith, hope and charity are the way to the mansions of the Father—Moroni says farewell and commends all to seek Jesus.",
 bg:["Verse 6 echoes Hebrews 11:1: “faith is the substance of things hoped for, the evidence of things not seen.” Verses 7–22 then follow the pattern of Hebrews 11, repeating “by faith” for one example after another. Verse 4 calls hope “an anchor to the souls of men,” a phrase close to Hebrews 6:19.","In 12:26–27 the Lord answers Moroni’s fear that his writing is weak: “my grace is sufficient for all men that humble themselves before me.” This is close to 2 Corinthians 12:9, where Paul also learns that the Lord’s grace is enough for his weakness. In verses 33–34 Moroni connects the Savior’s love for the world to “charity,” which Mormon defines further in Moroni 7:47."]},
"ether.13":{title:"A New Jerusalem upon this land",year:"Jaredite history, about 2000–600 BC",
 sum:"Ether teaches that this land is the place of the New Jerusalem, and that old Jerusalem will also be rebuilt—A remnant of Joseph will build the New Jerusalem, and the first shall be last—Cast out, Ether hides in a cavity of a rock and finishes the record—Coriantumr will not repent, and wars, robbers and bloodshed fill the land.",
 bg:["Verse 2 says the land became choice after “the waters had receded,” a reference to the flood in Genesis 8–9. The New Jerusalem is mentioned in Revelation 21:1–2, which shows it coming down from heaven. Moses 7:62–64 speaks of a holy city built by the righteous in the last days, and the tenth Article of Faith states that Zion “will be built upon the American continent.”","Verses 6–7 compare the Lord’s mercy to a remnant of Joseph’s family with Joseph’s going to Egypt, where he and his father later died. Verse 10 says its inhabitants are those “whose garments are white through the blood of the Lamb,” similar to Revelation 7:14. Verse 12 echoes the saying that the “first shall be last,” which Jesus gave in Matthew 19:30."]},
"ether.14":{title:"Who can stand before the army of Shiz?",year:"Jaredite history, about 2000–600 BC",
 sum:"A curse rests on the land so that tools and weapons disappear—Every man guards his own property with sword in hand—Coriantumr fights Shared’s brother Gilead, who is murdered by his high priest, then Lib, and Lib’s brother Shiz—Shiz burns cities and kills women and children—The land is covered with the dead—Coriantumr is badly wounded at the hill Comnor.",
 bg:["Verses 1–2 describe a land where trust has failed: a man’s tools might vanish overnight, so no one would “borrow neither would he lend.” This chapter is hard reading, and Moroni includes it to show what happens when people abandon God and trust in secret combinations (see 8:22–25).","The name Gilead is also a place in the Bible, east of the Jordan (Genesis 31:21). Its meaning there is not certain. The Book of Mormon does not give meanings for any of the names here, including Shared, Lib, Shiz and Coriantumr, though Coriantumr is also known from Omni 1:20–21."]},
"ether.15":{title:"The Spirit of the Lord had ceased striving",year:"Jaredite history, about 2000–600 BC",
 sum:"Coriantumr remembers Ether’s words, repents of the evil, and offers Shiz the kingdom to spare the people—Shiz refuses unless Coriantumr is given up, and the armies fight again at the waters of Ripliancum—Everyone gathers for a final battle, until only Coriantumr and Shiz are left—Coriantumr beheads Shiz and falls—Ether finishes and hides the record.",
 bg:["The waters of Ripliancum are said to mean “large, or to exceed all” (15:8). The text gives this as a translation of a Jaredite name. Verse 11 says the army camped by “the hill Ramah,” the same hill where Mormon hid the records (compare Mormon 6:6, where the Nephites gather at Cumorah). The Book of Mormon does not explain the name Cumorah.","In verse 19 “the Spirit of the Lord had ceased striving with them,” recalling Genesis 6:3, and Mormon says nearly the same thing about the Nephites in Moroni 8:28. Moroni 9:23 explains why he includes this account: the Nephites faced the same end “like unto the Jaredites.” Ether hid the plates so that the people of Limhi would find them (15:33; see Mosiah 8:7–9)."]},
"moro.1":{title:"I will not deny the Christ",year:"About AD 401–421",
 sum:"Moroni, after finishing the Jaredite abridgment, finds he has not yet perished and goes on writing—The Lamanites put to death every Nephite who will not deny Christ—Moroni will not deny Christ, wanders for his safety and writes a few more things for his brethren in a future day.",
 bg:["Moroni had thought his writing was done (Mormon 8:3–5). He shows that he is alone and in danger: he does not make himself known to the Lamanites lest they destroy him (1:1).","The book’s last chapters are not a long history but a handbook: how priests and teachers are ordained (Moroni 3), how the sacrament is blessed (Moroni 4–5) and what Mormon taught about faith, hope and charity (Moroni 7)."]},
"moro.2":{title:"Ye shall call on the Father in my name",year:"About AD 401–421",
 sum:"Moroni records the words Christ spoke to the twelve disciples as He laid His hands on them—They are to call on the Father in mighty prayer, then give the Holy Ghost to those on whom they lay hands—The multitude did not hear these words, but the Holy Ghost fell on all on whom the disciples laid their hands.",
 bg:["This passage describes what Jesus did at His first visit to the Nephites (see 3 Nephi 18:36–37, where He gives the disciples power to give the Holy Ghost). Moroni notes that the multitude did not hear these words (2:3).","The words “mighty prayer” (2:2) are plain: this was not casual speech but earnest, faithful asking. The pattern of laying on hands for the gift of the Holy Ghost is also in Acts 8:14–17 and 19:6."]},
"moro.3":{title:"I ordain you to be a priest",year:"About AD 401–421",
 sum:"Elders ordain priests and teachers by prayer and the laying on of hands—The words of ordination are given—Priests and teachers are to preach repentance and remission of sins through Christ—They are ordained according to the gifts and callings of God by the power of the Holy Ghost.",
 bg:["The short ordination prayer in verse 3 names the duty of priests and teachers: “to preach repentance and remission of sins through Jesus Christ, by the endurance of faith on his name to the end.” Doctrine and Covenants 20:46–60 describes the duties of elders, priests, teachers and deacons.","Moroni stresses that the ordination was “by the power of the Holy Ghost, which was in them” (3:4). He gives it as a plain pattern, so the Church could keep doing it after the Nephites were gone."]},
"moro.4":{title:"Always remember him",year:"About AD 401–421",
 sum:"Elders and priests administer the bread of the sacrament as Christ commanded—They kneel with the church and pray to the Father in Christ’s name—The bread is blessed to the souls of those who partake, in remembrance of the body of Christ, so they may witness they will take His name, always remember Him and keep His commandments, and have His Spirit.",
 bg:["This prayer is nearly the same as in Doctrine and Covenants 20:77. The sacrament goes back to the Last Supper (Matthew 26:26; 1 Corinthians 11:23–24) and to the Savior’s own sacrament among the Nephites (3 Nephi 18:1–7). The prayer is also a promise: those who partake witness that they are willing to take upon them the name of Christ.","The wording in this prayer, “that they may always have his Spirit to be with them,” links the ordinance with the gift of the Holy Ghost. The text gives the full prayer, so it can be used word for word."]},
"moro.5":{title:"In remembrance of the blood of thy Son",year:"About AD 401–421",
 sum:"The manner of administering the wine is given—The prayer asks the Father to bless and sanctify the wine to the souls of those who drink it in remembrance of the blood of Christ, which was shed for them—They witness that they always remember Him so they may have His Spirit.",
 bg:["This prayer matches Doctrine and Covenants 20:79 closely. The wording on wine follows the Savior’s own example in 3 Nephi 18:8–11. Today the Church uses water in the sacrament; in Doctrine and Covenants 27:2 the Lord says it “mattereth not what ye shall eat or what ye shall drink” when partaking, so long as it is done with an eye single to His glory.","Each prayer is short but complete: both ask for sanctification, both speak of remembrance, and both end with a promise of the Spirit. Members hear these two prayers each week at the sacrament table."]},
"moro.6":{title:"Numbered among the people of the church of Christ",year:"About AD 401–421",
 sum:"Baptism is given only to those who bring forth fruit and come with a broken heart and contrite spirit—They take upon them the name of Christ—The church meets often to fast, pray and partake of bread and wine—Members who sin are forgiven when they repent—Meetings are led by the Spirit.",
 bg:["The requirements for baptism in verses 1–3 are the same ones the Savior set in 3 Nephi 9:20 and 12:19, a “broken heart and a contrite spirit.” The names of baptized members were taken so they could be “remembered and nourished by the good word of God” (6:4). Doctrine and Covenants 20:37 gives a similar list.","In verse 7, a person who is found committing iniquity may be condemned “before the elders” by three witnesses; this resembles the process in Matthew 18:15–17. The same verses stress that “as oft as they repented and sought forgiveness, with real intent, they were forgiven” (6:8). Verse 9 says their meetings were led “by the power of the Holy Ghost.”"]},
"moro.7":{title:"Charity is the pure love of Christ",year:"About AD 401–421",
 sum:"Moroni records Mormon’s words about faith, hope and charity—Every thing that invites to do good is of Christ, and every thing that persuades to do evil is of the devil—Miracles and angels have not ceased, because faith has not ceased—Hope comes through the Atonement and the Resurrection, and requires meekness—Charity never faileth, for it is the pure love of Christ.",
 bg:["Mormon’s sermon begins with how to judge good from evil: “the Spirit of Christ is given to every man, that he may know good from evil” (7:16). He teaches that miracles do not stop because Christ ascended; they stop only if faith stops (7:27–38). The words in 7:5 echo Matthew 7:16–20, “by their fruits ye shall know them,” and 7:11 echoes James 3:11–12.","Verses 45–47 closely follow 1 Corinthians 13:4–8, 13. Mormon says charity “suffereth long, and is kind” as Paul does. He then adds the key sentence: “charity is the pure love of Christ.” The Greek word in Paul’s letter is agape, a word for self-giving love, and “charity” came into English through the Latin caritas, which translated it. Verse 48 invites us to pray to be filled with this love."]},
"moro.8":{title:"Little children are alive in Christ",year:"About AD 401",
 sum:"Mormon writes to Moroni soon after Moroni’s calling to the ministry—He is grieved by disputes over baptizing little children and has asked the Lord about it—Little children need no repentance or baptism because they are alive in Christ—Baptism is for the accountable who can repent—Mormon fears the Spirit has ceased striving with the Nephites.",
 bg:["Mormon says little children are redeemed through the Atonement: “Behold, I came into the world not to call the righteous but sinners to repentance” (8:8, echoing Matthew 9:13), and little children are not under a broken law (8:22–24). Compare Doctrine and Covenants 68:25–27, which says children should be baptized at eight, and 137:10, which says those who die before the age of accountability are saved. The second Article of Faith teaches that people are punished for their own sins.","Mormon’s words are strong because he is deeply concerned for children (8:17). Verses 25–26 describe the steps toward fullness of joy: faith, repentance, baptism, remission of sins, meekness, the Holy Ghost, hope and “perfect love.” Verse 28 says that “the Spirit hath ceased striving” with the Nephites, as Ether 15:19 says of the Jaredites."]},
"moro.9":{title:"Let us labor diligently",year:"About AD 401",
 sum:"Mormon writes again to Moroni of a sore battle in which the Nephites did not conquer—His people have lost their love and have no fear of death—He describes the cruelty of both Lamanites and Nephites with sorrow—He cannot recommend them to God but recommends Moroni, asking him to be faithful in Christ.",
 bg:["This is the saddest chapter in Moroni and contains painful accounts of war, which Mormon describes briefly and with sorrow. Mormon wrote these letters earlier than Moroni’s final record, and Moroni chose to include them. Mormon says, “let us labor diligently; for if we should cease to labor, we should be brought under condemnation” (9:6).","Mormon warns, “if they perish it will be like unto the Jaredites” (9:23), which ties this chapter to Ether 15. His closing words are a father’s blessing to his son: “may Christ lift thee up, and may his sufferings and death, and the showing his body unto our fathers, and his mercy and long-suffering, and the hope of his glory and of eternal life, rest in your mind forever” (9:25)."]},
"moro.10":{title:"Ask God, the Eternal Father, in the name of Christ",year:"About AD 401–421",
 sum:"Moroni writes to the Lamanites more than four hundred twenty years after the sign of Christ’s birth—Readers are invited to ask God if these things are true, and by the power of the Holy Ghost may know the truth of all things—The gifts of the Spirit are many and come from Christ—Faith, hope and charity are needed—Come unto Christ and be perfected in Him—Moroni bids farewell.",
 bg:["Verses 3–5 are the Book of Mormon’s well-known promise. Moroni asks readers to remember God’s mercy from Adam’s time, to ponder it, and then to ask God “with a sincere heart, with real intent, having faith in Christ.” This echoes James 1:5. The promise’s final line, “by the power of the Holy Ghost ye may know the truth of all things,” applies beyond the Book of Mormon.","The list of gifts in verses 8–17 follows the list in 1 Corinthians 12:4–11, with a few differences. Moroni includes “the beholding of angels and ministering spirits” (10:14) and lacks Paul’s “discerning of spirits.” Verse 31 draws on Isaiah 52:1–2 and 54:2, “put on thy beautiful garments” and “strengthen thy stakes.” In verses 32–33 Moroni teaches that Christ’s grace makes us perfect."]},
});
